"Jobs are posted on Monday" is one of those tips that gets repeated because it sounds
right. We looked at the day of the week employers gave to **10.7 million** postings
published between July and mid-November. Monday is not the peak.

## Which day of the week are most jobs posted?

| day | share of an average week's postings |
| --- | --- |
| Monday | 18.3% |
| Tuesday | 20.1% |
| Wednesday | 18.9% |
| Thursday | 18.8% |
| Friday | 17.7% |
| Saturday | 3.1% |
| Sunday | 3.0% |

Tuesday is the busiest day: about one posting in five goes up on a Tuesday. Monday,
Wednesday and Thursday sit close behind, Friday is the quietest weekday, and the gap between
them is a couple of points at most. The weekend is close to empty: about 6% of postings for
two days.

The pattern holds across hiring systems. Workday, Oracle, iCIMS, UKG and Paylocity all put
between 17% and 21% of their postings on each weekday and 1–3% on each weekend day.

## The Saturday that wasn't

Our first count said something else: 8.6% of postings on Saturday, more than twice the
figure above. All of that came from one hiring system. **SAP SuccessFactors** postings in our
data are dated Saturday 100% of the time — not because employers publish on Saturdays, but
because the date it gives is the day the posting's page last changed, and those pages
appear to be refreshed in one weekly batch.

So we leave SuccessFactors out, and Greenhouse too, whose date is the last time a posting
was edited. It is a reminder that a "posted" date is only as good as the system behind it.
A job board that shows you "posted 2 days ago" may be showing you the day of a refresh.

## Where the week looks different

Most countries look like the table above. A few do not.

| country | Mon | Tue | Wed | Thu | Fri | Sat | Sun |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| United States | 18.6% | 20.0% | 19.0% | 18.7% | 18.2% | 2.9% | 2.5% |
| United Kingdom | 17.5% | 19.2% | 19.0% | 19.7% | 17.4% | 3.7% | 3.4% |
| Germany | 16.8% | 19.1% | 16.7% | 18.2% | 18.1% | 5.9% | 5.2% |
| France | 19.4% | 20.0% | 17.7% | 17.6% | 20.8% | 2.6% | 1.9% |
| Brazil | 15.9% | 20.1% | 21.3% | 20.2% | 19.1% | 2.1% | 1.4% |
| India | 18.7% | 18.0% | 19.5% | 18.4% | 17.3% | 5.1% | 3.0% |
| Singapore | 17.9% | 19.3% | 17.9% | 17.7% | 17.7% | 5.0% | 4.6% |

Germany, Singapore and India put noticeably more on the weekend: 11%, 10% and 8% of their
postings, against 5.4% in the United States. France is the only large market where Friday
is the busiest day.

The clearest case is the Gulf. In Saudi Arabia and Qatar the weekend is Friday and
Saturday, and the data shows it: **14% and 18% of postings are dated Sunday**, a working day
there, and only about 6% Friday. The United Arab Emirates moved to a Saturday–Sunday weekend
in 2022, and its postings moved with it — 16% on Friday, 4% on Sunday.

## Do postings from some days close faster?

Postings dated on a weekend come down sooner: of those that have closed, half were gone
within about 20 days, against 26–28 days for postings from a weekday, and 14–15% closed
within a week against 8–11%. (These postings are all from July on, so the medians are
shorter than for postings of any age.) We would not read much into it. A large part of the
weekend postings come from job aggregators that republish others' listings under their own
date, and those close fast whichever day they appear.

Among weekdays the difference is small, and a posting coming down says nothing about
whether someone was hired.

## What it means for your search

- **Check for new postings on Tuesday to Thursday.** That is when most of them appear. A
  saved search that alerts you daily does this for you.
- **If you search once a week, do it at the end of the week.** By Friday afternoon most of
  the week's postings are up; on Monday morning you are looking at last week's.
- **Apply in the first week.** Of the postings that have closed, about one in ten came down
  within seven days of going up, whichever day it appeared.
- **Trust the employer's date over the board's.** A posting that says "today" on a board may
  be weeks old on the employer's site.

freehire shows the date the employer gave a posting, when there is one, and lets you
[save a search](/features/notifications) and get a daily digest of new matches.

## How we counted

We took postings published from 1 July to 15 November 2026 that carry a publication date
from the employer, counted each duplicate once, and grouped them by the day of that date.
That window has 19 Mondays and Tuesdays but 20 of every other day, so the weekday shares
are per day: each day's count divided by how many times it occurs, as a share of the week.
Most hiring systems give only a date; for those that give a time we used UTC, so a posting
published late on Friday evening in California counts as Saturday. Country figures use only
postings with a single country. Greenhouse and SAP SuccessFactors are left out for the
reasons above.

The data shows when postings appear, not when they get the most applicants or when
recruiters read them. We do not have that.
